  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "If $\\overset{―}{S}=\\{\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{7},\\sqrt{11}\\}$ and $\\overset{―}{T}=\\{\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{5}\\},$ then what is $\\overset{―}{S\\cup T}?$",
  "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for unions states that\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{S\\cup T}≡\\overset{―}{S}\\cap \\overset{―}{T}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{S\\cup T} & =\\overset{―}{S}\\cap \\overset{―}{T} \\\\  & =\\{\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{7},\\sqrt{11}\\}\\cap \\{\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{5}\\} \\\\  & =∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
